Dear all,

I am working on a longitudinal study involving a psychological intervention for individuals with chronic pain. Participants were randomly assigned to either an intervention group (n = 81) or a waitlist control group (n = 82). Data were collected at three timepoints:

·       T1: Baseline

·       T2: 8-week post-treatment

·       T3: 3-month follow-up

I am interested in testing mediation mechanisms of intervention effects overtime using lavaan.

Key constructs:

·       Predictor: Group (0 = waitlist, 1 = intervention)

·       Mediator: Perceived Stress Scale (PSS, 10 items)

·       Outcome: Depression (PHQ, 9 items)

Each construct is available as:

·       Sum scores

·       Item-level data
I conducted CFAs on each scale individually with good model fit and internal consistency, and established scalar invariance across timepoints.

My questions:

1.     Feasibility of using sum scores for mediation models:
My understanding is that modeling all constructs as latent variables at the item level (i.e., three latent variables per construct across time) may be too complex given my sample size.

o   Would it be acceptable to test longitudinal mediation using sum scores (i.e., as a path model) in a framework such as CLPM, RI-CLPM, or Latent Growth Curve Mediation?

2.     Alternatives to full latent modeling:
If full item-level latent modeling is not advisable, what are your thoughts on alternatives such as:

o   Parceling items

o   Estimating factor scores and applying Croon’s correction in mediation paths

o   Modeling only one construct as latent (e.g., the mediator), while treating predictor and outcome as manifest

3.     Less complex options for testing longitudinal mediation:
Are there less complex yet valid approaches to test mediation over time?
For example, is it acceptable to model PSS_T2 as a mediator of the intervention’s effect on PHQ_T3, controlling for PHQ_T1 an PSS_T1 and maybe controlling for PSS_T2 in lavaan?

I would be very grateful for your feedback on which modeling strategy is most appropriate and feasible in this context.

Kind regards,

Lola

